 "title": "NEWCOMB'S PARADOX",
 "angle": "If a near-perfect predictor has already filled the boxes, should you take one box or both?",
 "answer": "Two principles collide: the money is already placed, so taking both always adds $1,000, yet one-boxers almost always end up millionaires; people split almost evenly (53% one-box in a 31,000-person poll, 39% two-box vs 31% one-box among philosophers), and the real question is whether a choice that can be predicted is still yours.",
 "logline": "A near-perfect predictor has filled the boxes, so one or two? A physicist at a weapons lab set the rule: a million only if you'll take one box, but the money is already there, so two boxes always earns more, but one-boxers walk away rich, so when Nozick published it the world split almost in half, but predictors are now real, so the question becomes whether a predictable choice is still yours.",
 "hook_text": "$1,000 OR $1,000,000?",
 "hooks": [
  "Philosophers split almost in half over two boxes of money.",
  "A machine already knows which box you'll take."
 ],
 "world": {
  "scenery": "room"
 },
 "script": [
  {
   "text": "A glass box holds 1,000 dollars; the closed box, maybe a million.",
   "say": "A glass box holds a thousand dollars; the closed box, maybe a million.",
   "beat": "hook"
  },
  {
   "text": "A machine that's almost never wrong has already decided what's inside.",
   "beat": "hook",
   "link": "detail"
  },
  {
   "text": "So do you take both boxes, or just the closed one?",
   "beat": "question",
   "link": "so"
  },
  {
   "text": "A physicist at a California weapons lab invented this puzzle around 1960.",
   "say": "A physicist at a California weapons lab invented this puzzle around nineteen sixty.",
   "beat": "reveal1",
   "link": "answer"
  },
  {
   "text": "The rule: if the machine predicted you'd take only the closed box, it holds the million.",
   "beat": "reveal1",
   "link": "detail"
  },
  {
   "text": "If it predicted you'd take both, the closed box is empty.",
   "beat": "reveal1",
   "link": "detail"
  },
  {
   "text": "But the money is already in place; nothing you do now can change it.",
   "beat": "reveal2",
   "link": "but"
  },
  {
   "text": "So taking both always gets you 1,000 dollars more. Logic says: two boxes.",
   "say": "So taking both always gets you a thousand dollars more. Logic says: two boxes.",
   "beat": "reveal2",
   "link": "so"
  },
  {
   "text": "But people who take one box almost always walk away millionaires.",
   "beat": "reveal2",
   "link": "but"
  },
  {
   "text": "So when Robert Nozick published it in 1969, the world split.",
   "say": "So when Robert Nozick published it in nineteen sixty-nine, the world split.",
   "beat": "reveal3",
   "link": "so"
  },
  {
   "text": "Almost everyone, he wrote, finds the answer obvious, and the other half silly.",
   "beat": "reveal3",
   "link": "detail"
  },
  {
   "text": "In a 2016 online poll of 31,000 people, 53 percent took one box.",
   "say": "In a twenty sixteen online poll of thirty-one thousand people, fifty-three percent took one box.",
   "beat": "reveal3",
   "link": "detail"
  },
  {
   "text": "Among professional philosophers in 2020, two boxes led, 39 to 31.",
   "say": "Among professional philosophers in twenty twenty, two boxes led, thirty-nine to thirty-one.",
   "beat": "reveal3",
   "link": "but"
  },
  {
   "text": "But predictors are no longer science fiction: algorithms already forecast what you'll click and buy.",
   "beat": "turn",
   "link": "but"
  },
  {
   "text": "So the real question isn't the money; it's whether a choice that can be predicted is still yours.",
   "beat": "payoff",
   "link": "so"
  },
  {
   "text": "One box, or two?",
   "beat": "payoff",
   "link": "so"
  }
 ],
